{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>He shall put on the holy linen tunic</b> — that they are of the [materials reserved for] holiness, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 16:4). And He says, <b>\"they are holy garments\"</b> — that all of them are likewise of holiness. And in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, section 10): \"Holy he shall put on\" — that they are of [materials reserved for] holiness; this I know only regarding these alone, from where do I include the other garments of the High Priesthood and the garments of his brothers the priests? Scripture says, <b>\"they are holy garments\"</b> — a paradigm for all the garments, that they must be of holiness. And according to the plain sense, just as He said (Exodus 28:4), <b>\"And they shall make holy garments for Aaron your brother\"</b> — in the eight garments, so He says that these too are holy garments. And their mystery is like <b>\"the man clothed in linen\"</b> (Ezekiel 9:3, Daniel 10:5). And therefore He informed him that they are holy garments. And in Vayikra Rabbah (21:11): the fitness of those above is like the fitness of those below; just as the fitness of those above is one man among them clothed in linen, so the fitness of those below — <b>\"a holy linen tunic\"</b>." "<b>he shall put on, he shall gird, he shall wrap</b> — it is expounded in Torat Kohanim (Acharei 1:14): since we are about to include for him other garments between the two twilight periods, one might think he has no permission [to change] from those of the morning service — Scripture says, <b>\"he shall put on, he shall gird, he shall wrap.\"</b> And Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 16:4), of blessed memory, wrote: <b>\"יִצְנֹף\"</b> — \"יַחֵת בְּרֵישֵׁהּ,\" he shall place on his head, like <b>\"וַתַּנַּח בִּגְדוֹ אֶצְלָהּ\"</b> (Genesis 39:16) — <b>\"וְאַחֵתְתֵּהּ\"</b>. And the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 16:4) on this is not clear to me, for the צְנִיפָה is a winding that one rolls around his head like a צָנִיף, so why should he translate it with [a term for] placement alone, and not translate it with the term uniquely designated for צְנִיפָה itself? But perhaps we are not dealing with the Aramaic tongue, for also <b>\"the צְנִיפוֹת and the רְדִידִים\"</b> in Isaiah (3:23), Jonathan ben Uzziel translated as \"כִּתְרַיָּא\" (diadems), and neither of them has a term in Aramaic for צָנִיף and מִצְנֶפֶת, only the sacred tongue has <b>\"מַצְנַפְתָּא\"</b>."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The Hebrew מִשֶּׁל קוֹדֶשׁ, literally \"of holiness,\" refers here to materials or funds consecrated for sacred use; the phrase is left literal throughout.", 1 "Rashi's Aramaic glosses יַחֵת בְּרֵישֵׁהּ and וְאַחֵתְתֵּהּ are left untranslated as they are cited as Aramaic equivalents from Targum Onkelos; יַחֵת means \"he shall place\" and וְאַחֵתְתֵּהּ means \"and she placed it.\""}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:22.923342758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}